  "plan": {
    "goal": "Recapitulate the high wall shear stress of first-order human retinal arterioles in a perfused endothelial chip.",
    "rationale": "First-order human retinal arterioles (~100-150 µm diameter) experience unusually high wall shear stress relative to most microvascular beds, with literature values on the order of 10-50 dyn/cm² (1-5 Pa). I target 2 Pa (20 dyn/cm²) as a defensible mid-range value within this high-shear regime. A 100×100 µm square channel approximates the arteriolar lumen cross-section. Using the wall-shear relation tau = 6·mu·Q/(w·h²), a 20 µL/min flow delivers 2.0 Pa at medium viscosity 1e-3 Pa·s. This flow is laminar (Re ≈ 3.3), produces a modest 800 Pa pressure drop over 20 mm, and gives a 0.6 s residence time. HUVEC is the standard endothelial line for shear-response studies (registry shear range 0.1-1 Pa, so 2 Pa is a deliberate high-shear stress condition); a retinal-specific endothelial line (e.g. primary human retinal microvascular endothelial cells) would be more physiologically faithful and should be considered. Seeding at 2e4 cells/cm² over the 0.02 cm² channel floor yields ~400 cells. Oxygen supply at 20 µL/min vastly exceeds demand (ratio ~556×), so the high flow required for shear also guarantees oxygenation.",
